INT. AMBULANCE - MOVING - NIGHT

Blue light flickers across steel cabinets. A monitor beeps in a steady rhythm. The vehicle sways around a corner.

DELIA NAKAGAWA, 30s, a paramedic in a navy jumpsuit, kneels beside the stretcher with a blood pressure cuff. ODETTE FARADAY, 70s, lies on it under a gray blanket, a mask over her nose. She grips a small cloth purse against her ribs.

Delia checks the monitor. She frowns. She loosens the cuff. She pats her patient's arm.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over radio, crackling)
Medic Nine, status on your patient?

DELIA
(into radio)
Seventy-year-old female, chest pain, BP one-ten over seventy, sats at ninety-four.

MARCUS (V.O.)
Copy. ETA?

DELIA
Eight minutes.

MARCUS (V.O.)
Hospital's standing by.

She clips the radio back on her shoulder. She squeezes the older woman's hand. Odette's eyes drift open.

ODETTE
Did I faint?

DELIA
Not quite, Mrs. Faraday. You gave us a scare.

ODETTE
She tends to.

DELIA
Who does?

ODETTE
My daughter. Always rushing.

Delia glances at the purse. She raises an eyebrow, waiting. Odette tightens her grip on it. She turns her face toward the wall, and after a moment she lets her shoulders relax.

ODETTE (CONT'D)
I'm all right, dear.

DELIA
I know you are.

She tucks the blanket higher. The ambulance slows. She checks the monitor again and finally lets herself breathe.

MARCUS (V.O.)
(over radio)
Medic Nine, you're clear to enter bay two.

DELIA
Copy that.
